# Day 02 – Git & GitHub Practice Session

14. Key Lessons From Today's Mistakes

1) I learned that I don't need to clone the repository every day.
2) I only need to clone a repository when creating a local copy for the first time.
3) After that, I can continue working inside the existing local repository.
4) `git add` is used before `git commit`.
5) `git commit` saves staged changes locally.
6) `git push` uploads local commits to GitHub.
7) `git pull` gets changes from GitHub.
8) I learned how to unstage a file using `git restore --staged`.
9) I learned why filenames containing spaces or special characters should be placed inside quotes.
10) I learned how to check the repository state using `git status`.
11) I learned how to view commit history using `git log` and `git log --oneline`.
12) I practiced moving a branch back to an earlier commit using `git reset`.
13) I learned that a rejected push can happen when the remote repository contains commits that my local repository does not have.
14) I learned to synchronize with the remote repository before pushing when this happens.

* `cd` — Change the current directory.
* `cd ..` — Move one directory back.
* `ls` — List files and folders.
* `ls -a` — Show all files, including hidden files.
* `mkdir` — Create a new directory/folder.
* `touch` — Create a new empty file.
* `rm -f` — Remove a file.
* `rm -r` — Remove a directory recursively.
* `rm -rf` — Forcefully remove a directory and its contents.
* `cat` — Display the contents of a file.
* `cat > file.txt` — Create/overwrite a file with terminal input.
* `cat >> file.txt` — Append content to an existing file.
* `vi` — Open/edit a file from the terminal.
* `pwd` — Display the current working directory.
* `history` — Display previously executed Bash commands.
* `history > daytwo` — Save terminal command history into a file named `daytwo`.
* `cp` — Copy files or directories.
* `mv` — Move or rename files/directories.
